{"park":"capitol_reef","fetched":"2026-09-06T19:59:57+00:00","attribution":{"landmarks":"© OpenStreetMap contributors, ODbL","summaries":"Wikipedia, CC BY-SA 4.0","boundary":"iNaturalist place 69282"},"landmarks":[{"id":"node/356681337","name":"Cathedral Mountain","kind":"peak","lon":-111.3665,"lat":38.49281,"ele_m":2104.0,"wikidata":"Q49018691","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cathedral_Mountain_(Capitol_Reef_National_Park)","tour":0,"summary":{"extract":"Cathedral Mountain is a 6,924-foot (2,110 m) summit located in Capitol Reef National Park, in Wayne County of Utah, United States.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cathedral_Mountain_(Capitol_Reef_National_Park)","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","attribution":"Wikipedia"},"photos":[],"street":null},{"id":"node/356678320","name":"Ferns Nipple","kind":"peak","lon":-111.2129,"lat":38.24727,"ele_m":2153.0,"wikidata":"Q49028760","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ferns_Nipple","tour":3,"summary":{"extract":"Ferns Nipple is a 7,065-foot (2,153-meter) elevation summit located in Capitol Reef National Park, in Wayne County of Utah, United States. This iconic landmark is situated four miles (6.4 km) southeast of the park's visitor center. Precipitation runoff from this feature is drained by tributaries of the Fremont River, which in turn is within the Colorado River drainage basin. Folklore attributes this geological feature's naming to outlaw Butch Cassidy and his favored girlfriend named Fern.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ferns_Nipple","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","attribution":"Wikipedia"},"photos":[],"street":null},{"id":"node/356676347","name":"Temple of the Moon","kind":"peak","lon":-111.19142,"lat":38.44598,"ele_m":1726.0,"wikidata":"Q49081846","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_of_the_Moon_(Utah)","tour":1,"summary":{"extract":"Temple of the Moon is a 5,665-foot (1,727-meter) elevation summit located in Capitol Reef National Park, in Wayne County of Utah, United States. This remote, iconic monolith is situated 12 mi (19 km) north-northeast of the park's visitor center, and 0.37 mi (0.60 km) south of Temple of the Sun, in the Middle Desert of the park's North District. Cathedral Valley was so named in 1945 by Charles Kelly, first superintendent of Capitol Reef National Monument, because the valley's sandstone monoliths reminded early explorers of ornate, Gothic cathedrals, with fluted walls, alcoves, and pinnacles. The free-standing Temple of the Moon towers nearly 300 feet above its surrounding terrain, which is within the Fremont River drainage basin. John C. Frémont's 1853 expedition passed through Cathedral Valley.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_of_the_Moon_(Utah)","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","attribution":"Wikipedia"},"photos":[{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Temple_of_the_Moon_and_Lower_Cathedral_Valley_%283722870829%29.jpg/960px-Temple_of_the_Moon_and_Lower_Cathedral_Valley_%283722870829%29.jpg","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Temple_of_the_Moon_and_Lower_Cathedral_Valley_(3722870829).jpg","dist_m":49,"width":640,"height":427,"license":"CC BY-SA 2.0","license_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","artist":"Greg Willis from Denver, CO, usa"},{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Temple_of_the_Moon%2C_Cathedral_Valley.jpg/960px-Temple_of_the_Moon%2C_Cathedral_Valley.jpg","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Temple_of_the_Moon,_Cathedral_Valley.jpg","dist_m":89,"width":640,"height":480,"license":"CC BY-SA 2.0","license_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","artist":"Ken Lund"},{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Temple_of_the_Moon.jpg/960px-Temple_of_the_Moon.jpg","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Temple_of_the_Moon.jpg","dist_m":101,"width":640,"height":370,"license":"CC BY-SA 2.0","license_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","artist":"au_ears"},{"url":"https://upl